                                    <h2><span class="shaft"><img id="19-0" src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/icons/patterns/icon_c.png" width=27 height=27 alt="Cardioid" title="Cardioid" ></span>Audio-Technica AT4047/SV</h2>
                    
                    <h2 class="sub">                    Cardioid Condenser Microphone</h2>
                    <p>The AT4047 is a FET condenser with a fixed cardioid pickup pattern. On-mic switches provide access to a -10dB pad and a high-pass filter (-12dB/octave @ 80Hz). </p><p>The capsule is a center-terminated design with a 2-micron membrane whose active diameter is 21.4mm. The perforated metal plate covering the diaphragm is said to &#8220;[provide] enhanced element stability and optimal sensitivity;&#8221; it also acts as a passive acoustic equalizer.</p><p>A-T claims the mic&#8217;s transformer-coupled, true-condenser design creates a &#8220;rich, warm sound [that] takes you back to the classic FET studio microphones.&#8221; External reports suggest that, as the name implies, the AT4047/SV was intended to evoke the sound of the U-47 FET.</p><p><a rel="shadowbox" href="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images//mic_extras/audio-technica/AT4047-cutaway.jpg"><img src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/mic_extras/audio-technica/AT4047-cutaway-sm.jpg" class="thumb shaft" alt="AT4047/SV Cutaway" width="133" /></a>It bears a familial resemblance to the AT4033, also a cardioid condenser; the 4033 has an electret capsule and transformerless output.</p><p>In 2009, Audio-Technica released the AT4047MP, a tripolar version of the AT4047/SV. The MP version has slightly higher self-noise and lower sensitivity, but offers Omni and Figure-of-8 patterns in a similar transformer-coupled topology.</p><p>The mic includes an elastic shockmount.</p><blockquote><p><cite>Larry Crane, Tape Op</cite></p><p>On vocals there&#8217;s a breathy sort of intimacy that sits in the track perfectly for certain tunes.</p></blockquote><p></p><blockquote><p><cite>MIX</cite></p><p>The AT4047/SV comes with one of the best shock-mounts I&#8217;ve seen and has a first-rate fit and finish. My only gripe is that I would have preferred more than just a single polar pattern. What makes Audio-Technica&#8217;s new AT4047/SV so attractive is its ability to function well in many applications. It&#8217;s an outstanding mic for vocal and instrumental recording-exhibiting a warm, full sound with plenty of depth and detail. Further, the microphone handles surprisingly high SPLs with ease and is awesome for ambience recording when used as a pair. Considering the AT4047/SV has a suggested retail price of $695, acquiring a pair is not so farfetched. I&#8217;d be thrilled to have three or four.</p></blockquote><p><p>The <a href="//recordinghacks.com/microphones/Audio-Technica/AT4047-SV">Audio-Technica AT4047/SV</a> is also known as: AT4047-SV, AT4047SV, 4047SV, AT4047, AT-4047.</p>
